## What Problem This Solves

Follow-up hardening for #100456 and #99712. A completed interactive turn must never disappear without a visible terminal reply, but progress notices, blank metadata, queued send policies, explicit silence, message-tool delivery, and pending continuations must not be mistaken for completion.

## Why This Change Was Made

The first fix established the empty-reply fallback. Reading the full direct and queued delivery paths exposed nine coupled invariants that needed one canonical treatment:

- distinguish visible output from committed outbound delivery;
- keep queued delivery inside normal send-policy enforcement;
- treat compaction notices as progress, not terminal replies;
- carry fallback payloads through normal metadata, routing, and reply-to preparation;
- reject whitespace-only and contentless payloads as visible output;
- keep disabled reasoning/commentary lanes from consuming delivery bookkeeping;
- preserve explicit silence, message-tool-only delivery, and pending continuations;
- hold the queued reply lane until asynchronous recovery delivery settles; and
- preserve group/channel silent-reply policy for the generated failure text.

The refactor removes the duplicate fallback helper and keeps failure copy plus conversation policy in the existing agent-runner execution owner.

## User Impact

Interactive direct and queued turns now either produce a genuinely visible response, complete a committed delivery, or emit the normal policy-aware failure reply. Hidden progress, blank payloads, suppressed lanes, or asynchronous dispatcher timing can no longer make a turn appear complete prematurely.

## Evidence

- Blacksmith Testbox exact-head focused matrix: 5 unit-fast tests, 456 auto-reply tests, and 86 end-to-end tests passed (547 total).
- Blacksmith Testbox path-scoped `pnpm check:changed` covered all 14 touched files.
- Fresh branch autoreview: clean, no accepted/actionable findings (confidence 0.88).
- `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` passed.

Refs #100456
